UNESCO Guidance on Artificial Intelligence in Education

UNESCO published guidance in 2023 to help governments, educational institutions, teachers, and researchers understand the opportunities and risks associated with generative artificial intelligence in education.

The guidance explains that generative AI may support curriculum design, teaching, learning, and research. These tools can help people create educational materials and explore new ways of presenting information. However, AI should not be treated as a complete solution to the challenges facing education systems.

UNESCO emphasizes a human-centred approach to AI. Educational technology should support human agency, inclusion, equity, and cultural and linguistic diversity. Teachers and learners should retain meaningful control over educational decisions.

The guidance identifies concerns involving privacy, safety, copyright, and the possibility of harmful or misleading outputs. It recommends appropriate policies, safeguards, and capacity-building measures so that educational institutions can evaluate AI tools before adopting them.

Teachers and researchers need support and training to use generative AI effectively and responsibly. Educational institutions should consider whether a tool is pedagogically appropriate, ethically acceptable, and suitable for the learners who will use it.

UNESCO also highlights the importance of protecting personal data and considering age-appropriate use. Governments and institutions should develop suitable rules and policies rather than assuming that AI tools are automatically safe or beneficial.

The guidance recognizes that generative AI may create opportunities for education while also introducing risks. Its overall message is that AI should be used thoughtfully, with human judgement, responsible governance, and attention to the needs and rights of learners.

